WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.530 --> 00:00:04.450
Lens flare is everywhere.

00:00:04.450 --> 00:00:10.629
It glows on our Supermen, near our Iron Men,
and around our method-acted oilmen.

00:00:10.629 --> 00:00:16.550
It’s in the fifth dimension, the final frontier,
and definitely the fictional town of Lillian,

00:00:16.550 --> 00:00:17.550
Ohio.

00:00:17.550 --> 00:00:25.750
The lens flare is a technical phenomenon and
distinct sensibility — that’s taken over.

00:00:25.750 --> 00:00:27.810
Lens flares can get pretty complicated.

00:00:27.810 --> 00:00:31.920
There are countless lenses from the past century
of filmmaking, not to mention the physics

00:00:31.920 --> 00:00:32.920
involved.

00:00:32.920 --> 00:00:36.350
But a movie fan can identify flare pretty
quickly.

00:00:36.350 --> 00:00:41.000
The basics are that each lens has a bunch
of parts, and when bright light shines at

00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:45.649
the right intensity or angle, it can bounce
around in those parts.

00:00:45.649 --> 00:00:50.350
That bouncing produces a bright haze as well
as lens flare.

00:00:50.350 --> 00:00:54.590
The flare’s shape depends on how diaphragm
blades — these things — close to create

00:00:54.590 --> 00:00:57.409
the aperture, which is where light gets in.

00:00:57.409 --> 00:01:00.680
If they have fewer blades, you might get a
hexagon-shaped flare.

00:01:00.680 --> 00:01:03.140
More blades and it’ll be closer to a circle.

00:01:03.140 --> 00:01:05.229
Anamorphic lenses have a different flare.

00:01:05.229 --> 00:01:10.540
They’re designed with an oval-shape to squish
more information onto a piece of 35 millimeter

00:01:10.540 --> 00:01:11.560
film.

00:01:11.560 --> 00:01:14.640
When they’re projected out with another
lens, it’s in a wider format.

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:19.750
And all that makes lens flares that can be
like stripes of light across the picture.

00:01:19.750 --> 00:01:21.930
But these flares weren’t always so trendy.

00:01:21.930 --> 00:01:25.300
For decades, cinematographers fought to hide
them.

00:01:25.300 --> 00:01:30.090
When Gregg Toland shot Citizen Kane, he and
director Orson Welles innovated — constantly.

00:01:30.090 --> 00:01:35.172
Famously, they used deep focus to show a lot
of stuff clearly and at the same time.

00:01:35.172 --> 00:01:39.230
They increased the depth of field by making
the aperture tiny and using really really

00:01:39.230 --> 00:01:40.230
bright lights.

00:01:40.230 --> 00:01:45.679
So, Toland coated his lenses with Vard Opticoat
to reduce glare and lens flare from all that

00:01:45.679 --> 00:01:46.679
light.

00:01:46.679 --> 00:01:49.320
And it set a norm and a path for innovation.

00:01:49.320 --> 00:01:53.299
Lenses were constantly coated with new and
better technologies that helped keep light

00:01:53.299 --> 00:01:55.710
from bouncing around and creating flare.

00:01:55.710 --> 00:02:00.950
It also set an expectation: to make an immersive,
professional movie, you did not have flare

00:02:00.950 --> 00:02:04.250
— until a revolution in moviemaking changed
it.

00:02:04.250 --> 00:02:08.900
In the 1960s, new filmmakers wanted to show
their movies weren’t made in a box.

00:02:08.900 --> 00:02:12.280
They turned to flare to capture a documentary-like
look.

00:02:12.280 --> 00:02:17.159
Legendary cinematographer Conrad L. Hall’s
work in Cool Hand Luke was a rebellion that

00:02:17.159 --> 00:02:18.519
turned into a movement.

00:02:18.519 --> 00:02:23.110
He even talked about it in 1992’s Visions
of Light: The Art of Cinematography.

00:02:23.110 --> 00:02:28.000
“I feel particularly involved in helping
make mistakes acceptable.

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:35.700
If the light shone in the lens and flared
the lens, that was considered a mistake.

00:02:35.700 --> 00:02:40.470
Somebody would report that, the operator would
report — oh, it hit the lens, it flared

00:02:40.470 --> 00:02:41.470
the lens.

00:02:41.470 --> 00:02:42.470
Cut!”

00:02:42.470 --> 00:02:46.900
He showed the heat of chain gang life by keeping
in the flare, and that quickly spread to rebel

00:02:46.900 --> 00:02:49.569
movies like The Graduate and Easy Rider.

00:02:49.569 --> 00:02:55.190
They symbolized authenticity and, as a result,
lens flares did too.

00:02:55.190 --> 00:03:00.120
Just as flare helped sell the reality of characters,
it could sell a sense of wonder.

00:03:00.120 --> 00:03:05.620
Flare spread really quickly, like in 1968’s
Planet of the Apes: “You maniacs!”

00:03:05.620 --> 00:03:10.610
And later, Steven Spielberg brought it into
Close Encounters of the Third Kind.

00:03:10.610 --> 00:03:13.129
Flare had entered the sci-fi and action canon.

00:03:13.129 --> 00:03:17.510
If early flare said, “these feelings are
real,” now it said, “these spacemen and

00:03:17.510 --> 00:03:19.180
explosions are real, too.”

00:03:19.180 --> 00:03:20.180
And though J.J.

00:03:20.180 --> 00:03:25.269
Abrams gets slammed for his gratuitous lens
flare use, its use as a tool of realism and

00:03:25.269 --> 00:03:30.269
wonder has impressed everyone from schlock
jocks like Michael Bay to true artistes from

00:03:30.269 --> 00:03:31.940
Sofia Coppola to Terrence Malick.

00:03:31.940 --> 00:03:33.610
Malick can’t get enough.

00:03:33.610 --> 00:03:38.519
It’s in videogames, political logos, and
maybe your phone’s weather screen.

00:03:38.519 --> 00:03:43.150
Movies in the 60s had to prove they weren’t
made in this box, and in the 90s they used

00:03:43.150 --> 00:03:47.220
lens flare to try to prove they weren’t
made in this one, even though flare’s become

00:03:47.220 --> 00:03:50.440
available with just a few clicks.

00:03:50.440 --> 00:03:52.939
Lens flare used to be a mistake you tried
to avoid.

00:03:52.939 --> 00:03:57.350
Now, it’s a choice you can’t escape.

00:03:57.350 --> 00:04:01.849
So you’ve seen a lot of computer generated
lens flare today, but all of this stuff is

00:04:01.849 --> 00:04:02.939
actually legitimate.

00:04:02.939 --> 00:04:07.530
However, to get it to show up reliably, I
had to place the light really close to the

00:04:07.530 --> 00:04:08.530
camera.

00:04:08.530 --> 00:04:12.969
That’s because modern lenses have a protective
coating that’s pretty good at stopping flare.

00:04:12.969 --> 00:04:17.440
It’s also the reason that cinematographers
on movies like There Will Be Blood and Saving

00:04:17.440 --> 00:04:23.060
Private Ryan have actually stripped away that
coating just so that they can get the old

00:04:23.060 --> 00:04:24.129
school flare look.

